WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:10.660
This project is about a dataset created through
a joint effort between MIT and Facebook.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.049
As it turns out, this dataset is way more
useful than I initially thought, I'll tell

00:00:15.049 --> 00:00:17.010
you in a moment why.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.900
Datasets are used to train and test the quality
of learning algorithms.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.779
This particular dataset contains short video
clips.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:29.939
These clips are passed to a neural network
which is asked to classify the kind of activity

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:31.830
that is taking place in the video.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:37.160
In this dataset, there are many cases where
everything is given to come to a logical answer

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.320
that is wrong.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.700
We may be in a room with the climbing wall,
but exercising is not necessarily happening.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.940
We could be around the swimming pool, but
swimming is not necessarily happening.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.830
I am pretty sure this has happened to you
too.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:56.450
This is a brilliant idea, because it is super
easy for a neural network to assume that if

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:02.220
there is a swimming pool, swimming is probably
happening, but it takes a great deal of understanding

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.480
to actually know what constitutes the swimming
part.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.780
A few episodes ago, we discussed that this
could potentially be a stepping stone towards

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.950
creating machines that think like humans.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.730
Without looking into it, it would be easy
to think that creating a dataset is basically

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:22.320
throwing a bunch of training samples together
and calling it a day.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.980
I can assure you that this is not the case
and that creating a dataset like this was

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:33.110
a herculean effort as it contains more than
half a million videos, and almost two million

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.050
annotations for 200 different activities.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:41.720
And, there are plenty of pre-processing steps
that one has to perform to make it usable.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.290
The collection procedure contains a video
crawling step where a large number of videos

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:51.350
are obtained from YouTube, which are to be
deduplicated, which means removing videos

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.549
that are too similar to one already contained
in the database.

00:01:55.549 --> 00:01:59.710
A classical case is many different kinds of
commentary on the same footage.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:04.790
This amounted to the removal of more than
150 thousand videos.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:10.289
Then, all of these videos undergo a shot and
person detection step where relevant subclips

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.090
are extracted that contain some kind of human
activity.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:18.769
These are then looked at by two different
classifiers, and depending on whether there

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:23.889
was a consensus between the two, a decision
is made whether the clip is to be discarded

00:02:23.889 --> 00:02:24.889
or not.

00:02:24.889 --> 00:02:30.180
This step helps balancing the ratio of videos
where there is some sort of relevant action

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.829
compared to clips where there is no relevant
action happening.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.499
This also makes the negative samples much
harder because the context may be correct,

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:41.129
but the expected activity may not be there.

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:45.709
This is the classical hard case with the swimming
pool and people in swimming suits twiddling

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:48.090
their thumbs instead of swimming.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.849
And here comes the more interesting part - when
trying to train a neural network for other,

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:58.959
loosely related tasks, using this dataset
for pre-training improves the scores significantly.

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:03.409
I'll try to give a little context for the
numbers, because these numbers are absolutely

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:04.480
incredible.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.309
There are cases where the success rate is
improved by over 30%, which speaks for itself.

00:03:10.309 --> 00:03:16.700
However, there are other cases where the difference
is about 10-15% percent, that is also remarkable

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:22.239
when we are talking about high numbers because
the closer the classifier gets to a 100%,

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:26.590
the more difficult the remaining corner cases
are that improve the accuracy.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:30.379
In these cases, even a 3% improvement is remarkable.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:35.340
And before we go, greetings and best regards
to Lucas, the little scholar who seems to

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.739
be absorbing the papers along with the mother's
milk.

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:39.739
Excellent.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:42.599
You can't start early enough in the pursuit
of knowledge.

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:46.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

